<Book title="Quantum Algorithms, Step by Step" current="orientation" sections={sections}>
<div class="breadcrumb">The textbook / Orientation</div><span class="eyebrow">A WORKED PATH FROM FIRST QUERY TO MODERN ALGORITHMS</span><h1>Quantum algorithms,<br/>one calculation at a time.</h1><p class="dek">Start with a problem. Follow the amplitudes. Open a missing definition, calculate a small example, and return to the algorithm.</p>
<div id="resume-reading" hidden class="context-box"></div>
<div class="lesson-metadata"><span>{lessons.length} numbered topics</span><span>Arithmetic + basic programming</span><span>No account or cloud service</span></div>
<div class="prose">
<h2 id="start-with-an-algorithm">Start with an algorithm</h2><p>The first two lessons are <a href="/lessons/deutsch/">01. Deutsch</a> and <a href="/lessons/grover/">02. Grover</a>. Each introduces the mathematics it needs inside the example. Deutsch turns a relative sign into a bit. Grover turns one marked sign into a high-probability label. Calculate those transformations before learning their general names.</p><p>You do not need a prior linear-algebra course. You do need to stop at the occasional four-entry column and multiply it out. The goal is to understand what an operation does, why it is needed, and how its result reaches a measurement.</p>
<h2 id="the-reading-loop">The depth-first reading loop</h2><ol><li><strong>Keep the question visible.</strong> What input is hidden, what output do we need, and what access is allowed?</li><li><strong>Read the next operation.</strong> Predict what changes, including what remains unchanged.</li><li><strong>Descend only as needed.</strong> A link such as <a href="/definitions/tensor-products/">tensor product</a> opens an explanation over the lesson. Nested links preserve a return stack. The original lesson and scroll position remain intact.</li><li><strong>Return and substitute.</strong> Put the calculated result back into the original state. A detour ends when you can explain or predict that step.</li><li><strong>Complete the example.</strong> Follow it through measurement and any classical postprocessing. A circuit diagram without its output is not a finished explanation.</li></ol><p>The <a href="/path/">main path</a> is a suggested traversal, not an access lock. Every chapter is directly accessible. The <a href="/prerequisites/">prerequisite map</a> shows conceptual dependencies. Optional branches deepen a family without interrupting the core sequence.</p>
<h2 id="conventions">Notation and conventions</h2><p>States are column vectors. The computational basis is in ascending binary order: for two bits, 00, 01, 10, 11. The leftmost register is written first and its leftmost bit is most significant. A local bit index 0 refers to the leftmost bit. Matrices act right-to-left: in ABv, B acts first. A dagger means conjugate transpose; a prime usually labels a new value and is explicitly defined when used.</p><p>The QFT uses a positive complex exponential; its inverse uses the negative sign. Quantum software packages may display bit order differently. This site uses its own small simulator and never silently imports a library's ordering. Equality signs that rewrite a state are not gates. Negative amplitudes are not negative probabilities.</p><p>The first lessons use real numbers; complex numbers appear when phase needs more than a plus or minus sign. Later chapters introduce mixed states, observables, spectra, and singular values with concrete matrices. The <a href="/glossary/">definition library</a> is available on demand, not assigned as a prerequisite course.</p>
<h2 id="what-counts-as-learning">What counts as learning</h2><p>Reproduce the fixed example with definitions available. Then change one operation, input, or assumption and predict the result. Reveal the explained exercise answer only after making a prediction. The progress buttons record <em>your</em> assessment: read, practiced, or revisit. Scrolling never marks understanding.</p><p>The demonstrations expose exact amplitudes because they are classical simulations, capped at eight qubits. Physical devices return measurement outcomes; they do not reveal the state vector. Sampled outcomes are visibly separate from ideal probabilities. Nothing on this site is presented as a hardware experiment.</p>
<h2 id="scope">Scope and evidence</h2><p>This is a broad, finite curriculum, not a claim to include every published quantum algorithm. The <a href="/coverage/">coverage audit</a> separates algorithms, primitives, frameworks, protocols, and applications; lists exact editorial gaps; and records sources checked on 2 November 2026. Authored chapters are distinct from independently reviewed chapters.</p><p>Every speedup has an access model and output contract. One oracle call may hide an expensive circuit. A quantum linear solver usually returns a quantum state, not a printed vector. Variational methods do not carry a general practical-speedup guarantee. These distinctions are checked in the worked examples, not used as substitutes for explaining them.</p>
